#ODL Netconf

#Assumption: odl-restconf feature is already installed.
#ODL supports 2 types of Northbound Netconf plugins that can be used to interact with it.

#	1. NETCONF server for ODL config-subsystem (listening on port 1830) . This is not supported starting with the Boron release and is eventually deprecated.
#		
#	2. NETCONF server for MD-SAL (listening by default on port 2830)
#		This acts as an alternative Northbound interface for MD-SAL (besides RESTCONF) and allows users to interact with ODL .
#		It is also possible for ODL to mount its own md-sal subsystem as a netconf node to  read/write data from MD-SAL’s datastore and to invoke its rpcs.
#		The northbound MDSAL netconf plugin is activated by installing feature odl-netconf-mdsal


#There are two ways for ODL to connect to netconf nodes using a southbound plugin.

1. Using the controllerconfig subsystem loopback mount point Northbound plugin and mount netconf nodes using it. Need to mount controller config subsystem as a netconf node first.
2. Directly using MDSAL network topology <- preferred method.  This will be the only method available in future releases. We use this option.
	This option is activated by installing odl-netconf-topology or odl-netconf-clustered-topology package <- recommended.  			
			
#ODL Netconf set up.
# Install Netconf client southbound plugin:
	feature:install odl-netconf-clustered-topology 
	
#Install ODL Northbound MDSAL Netconf server
	feature:install odl-netconf-mdsal
	
# Verify installed packages
feature:list -i
